  "query_preview": "(Saturday through Early Next Week)... \n\nAs we head into the weekend, ensemble and multimodal guidance \ndepict considerably more noise than signal regarding additional \nprecipitation chances. Ultimately, the broader synoptic pattern \nsupports a return to drier weather by Saturday. Subsidence will \nwork in behind the departing mid-week shortwave energy, allowing\nthe prominent upper-level heat ridge anchored off to our west \nto expand back eastward into the Mid-Mississippi River Valley.\n\nAs the ridge builds in, uncomfortable summertime heat seems \npoised to make a return. Highs will push back into the low 90s, \nand with lingering boundary layer moisture, heat index values \nwill creep back near the 100-degree mark by early next week. The\none major caveat to this heat will be our position on the \nperiphery of the ridge. There is more than a remote possibility \nthat subtle wrinkles in the mid-level flow crest the ridge, \nbringing thunderstorm complexes (a classic \"ring of fire\" \nsetup) into central Illinois. Should these complexes \nmaterialize, they would easily thwart the heat forecast, making \ntemperature trends highly dependent on mesoscale convective \ntrends early next week.",
